  "text": "At x=1,\n- 1^2 = (q(1))^2 + 32 (Multiply by 2)\n- (q(1))^2 = -33\n=> (q(1))^2 = 33\n=> q(1) = (33^0.5) or (-33^0.5)\n\nAt x=2,\n- 1^4 = (q(2))^2 + 32 (Multiply by 2)\n- (q(2))^2 = -27\n=> q(2) = (27^0.5) or (-27^0.5)\n=> q(2) = 3sqrt(3) or -3sqrt(3)\n\nAt x=3,\n- 1^12 = (q(3))^2 + 32 (Multiply by 3)\n- (q(3))^2 = -1\n=> q(3) = sqrt(1) or -sqrt(1)\n=> q(3) = 1 or -1\n\n####Step 3: Use polynomial interpolation\n\nAfter x = 1 and x = 2, the polynomial q(x) can be estimated using the three points obtained thus far:\n\na) Taking (1,3sqrt(3)), (2,3sqrt(3)), and (3,1)\n\nThe linear interpolation between points (1,3sqrt(3)) and (2,3sqrt(3)) can be approximately substituted for the future values of x:\n\nb) Interpolate points (1,3sqrt(3)) and (3,1)\n\nSimilarly,\n\nc)f'(x)=3(2)x\n\n####Step 4: Polynomial interpolation\n\nHere,\n\nFrom step 3, we have:\n\na) f(1)= (16*1^2+32)^0.5\nb) f(3)= 0\nc)f'(x)=-3x(2)^0.5\n\n####Step 5: Polynomial q(x)\n\nIf we assume the general form of p(x) is p(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d,\n\nWe have a = d, b = c, and d = root(f(x)) or the square root of f(x).\n\nOn comparing,\nf(1) and 1, we get the equation for the peak and 0, and finding the value of x ∨ the parameters a and c.\n\n####Step 6: Implement code to normalize the polynomial\n\nFirst, implement a function to evaluate q(x):\n\\[q(x) = x^4 + 4x^3 + 2x^2 + 8x + d\\]\nThen find the value of d such that:\n\\[q(1) = 2x\\sqrt{2\\sqrt{3}-33},\\]\n\n####Step 7: Check for further assumptions\n\nContinuing,\n\\[q(x^4) = x^8 + 4x^4 + 2x^2 + 8x + d\\]\n\\[q(x^4 - 4) = x^8 - 4x^4 + 2x^2 - 8x + d\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[q(x^4) - q(x^4 - 4) = (x^8 + 4x^4 + 2x^2 + 8x + d)-(x^8 - 4x^4 + 2x^2 - 8x + d)\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get the constant left in the polynomial term, which can be trivially zero, which in turn helps in approximating the shape of the function. Hence, we can ignore further details for computation-based stuff.",
